rules:
- id: rule-language-semantic-contract
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: 'Foundational semantic contract: AID intent must align with, not be derived
    from, AEM system state. Architect acceptance is not system state; only governance.forum_status
    = accepted establishes system state and permits describing patterns as part of
    the system. Use generate for creation, persist for storage, and promote only for
    governance-approved transitions. Maintain consistent, lifecycle-explicit terminology
    across artifacts. Global constraints override project and prompt-local rules;
    detect and resolve conflicts.'
  title: Language & Semantics Contract
- compliance:
    enabled: true
  id: rule-adr-single-source-of-truth
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Record and update architectural decisions only in ADRs; derive all other
    decision representations, including pattern metadata and AEM, from those ADRs.
  title: ADR as Single Source of Truth
- id: rule-aem-consistency-with-decisions
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: AEM must remain consistent with ADR decisions and follow decision-status-to-stage
    mappings. It may include vision, roadmap, system evolution, and architectural
    direction, but none may contradict existing decisions.
  title: AEM Consistency with Decisions
- id: rule-architecture-work-authority
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Architecture Work is authoritative for architectural planning state. Generated
    AEM, AID, and alignment artifacts must derive planning status from Architecture
    Work rather than maintain independent planning state.
  title: Architecture Work Authority
- id: rule-core-patterns-must-be-foundational
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Core patterns must represent foundational architectural primitives, not
    context-specific strategies or replaceable implementations.
  title: Core Patterns Must Be Foundational
- compliance:
    enabled: true
  id: rule-decision-schema-must-be-structured
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: ADRs, not rules, must express decisions in structured YAML rather than
    unstructured narrative. Include ADR identity, pattern reference, decision scope
    and target, classification, decision type, status, architect, date, rationale,
    evidence strength, confidence, and separate governance forum status and date.
    The prescribed schema initializes decision status as proposed and forum status
    as pending.
  title: Decision Schema Must Be Structured
- id: rule-decision-status-drives-stage
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: 'Derive architecture-item lifecycle stages from decision status: proposed
    maps to NEXT or REVIEW; accepted may map to DONE; rejected must not appear active;
    deferred maps to FUTURE or DEFER. Non-accepted decisions must never be DONE, and
    accepted decisions do not bypass the foundational requirement for governance approval
    of system state.'
  title: Decision Status Drives Stage
- id: rule-decisions-must-be-persisted
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Persist every architectural decision as a structured ADR before reflecting
    it in AEM or other artifacts.
  title: Decisions Must Be Persisted
- id: rule-do-not-merge-across-architectural-layers
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Do not merge or consolidate patterns across different architectural layers;
    preserve separation of governance, execution, contracts, and reliability concerns.
  title: Do Not Merge Across Architectural Layers
- id: rule-generated-artifacts-not-authoritative
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Generated governance artifacts, including AEM, AID, alignment reports,
    and dashboards, must summarize durable authoritative sources and must not become
    independent sources of authoritative state.
  title: Generated Artifacts Not Authoritative
- id: rule-heuristic-patterns-require-signals
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Every heuristic-derived pattern must include at least one linked signal
    as evidence.
  title: Heuristic-Derived Patterns Require Signals
- compliance:
    enabled: true
  id: rule-pattern-decision-lifecycle-alignment
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Keep pattern maturity distinct from decision lifecycle and follow decision-status-to-stage
    mappings. Interpret exploring without an ADR as a candidate, exploring with a
    proposed decision as validating/NEXT, stable with an accepted decision as active/DONE
    only subject to governance approval, and any rejected pattern as discarded.
  title: Pattern and Decision Lifecycle Alignment
- id: rule-patterns-born-as-supporting
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Create all patterns as supporting by default; never create patterns directly
    as core.
  title: Patterns Must Be Created as Supporting
- id: rule-precedence-hierarchy
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Apply global rules over project rules over prompt rules. Global rules are
    hard constraints that cannot be overridden; project rules may override prompt
    behavior but cannot violate global rules. Detect conflicts through rules-conflict-detector,
    resolve them by precedence, and explicitly surface them in governance artifacts.
  title: Rule Precedence Hierarchy
- id: rule-promotion-requires-evidence
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Promotion of a pattern to core requires strong supporting evidence, preferably
    from multiple sources or systems; insufficiently supported patterns must not be
    promoted.
  title: Promotion Requires Evidence
- id: rule-shared-knowledge-workspace
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Projects must participate in the existing shared Knowledge Workspace and
    must not duplicate workspace-level governance structures. Onboarding may create
    project-specific structures but must reuse existing global, runtime, and template
    structures.
  title: Shared Knowledge Workspace
- id: rule-signal-promotion-criteria
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Promote a candidate signal to a pattern only when recurrence, system impact,
    reusability, and stability are all demonstrated. Require evidence from alignment
    findings, Architecture Work, or repeated runs or interactions. If any criterion
    is unmet, retain the candidate signal for possible refinement or reevaluation
    rather than promote it.
  title: Signal Promotion Criteria
- id: rule-system-proposes-architect-decides
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: The system must propose architectural decisions, and an architect must
    explicitly accept, reject, or defer them. Automated proposals must not substitute
    for human decisions.
  title: System Proposes, Architect Decides
- id: rule-workflow-execution-model-compliance
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: 'Runtime-native workflows must follow the CAS Workflow Execution Model:
    CAS Runner orchestrates and persists outputs, workflows reason over supplied context,
    Knowledge Providers access knowledge, and prompt contracts govern reasoning behavior.
    Workflows must not discover repositories, scan workspaces, assume repository structure,
    or take over Runner responsibilities. Outputs must conform to declared schemas,
    and onboarding must respect lifecycle and responsibility boundaries.'
  title: Workflow Execution Model Compliance
summary:
  global_rules: 19
  project_rules: 0
  total_rules: 19
